user messages chatbot >>> chatbot sends requests to the server >>> server will send the request to Anthropic API

Every interaction with Claude follows a predictable pattern with five distinct phases:
1. Client's request to server
2. Server's request to Anthropic API
3. Model processing
4. Anthropic API's response to server
5. Server's Response to client.

Point to note
Never make requests to the Anthropic API directly from client-side code because API requests require a secret API key for authentication and Exposing this key in client code creates a serious security vulnerability.
Instead, your web or mobile app sends requests to your own server, which then communicates with the Anthropic API using the securely stored key.

Working with Anthropic API
--------------------------
How does server communicates with Anthropic API:
You can use either an official SDK (published by Anthropic) (Anthropic provides SDKs for Python, TypeScript, JavaScript, Go, and Ruby.)
Or make plain HTTP requests. 

Every request must include these 4 essential fields:
-------------------------------------------------------
API Key - Identifies your request to Anthropic
Model - Name of the model to use (like "claude-3-sonnet")
Messages - List containing the user's input text
Max Tokens - Limit for how many tokens Claude can generate


Anthropic API working - Text generation
-----------------------------------------
Text generation process by Anthropic API 
Tokenization                  --> User text is broken down into tokens (lets say one word one token)
Embedding                     --> Embedding is generated for each token (multiple possible meanings for each token)
Contextualization             --> Embedding is refined and adjusted according to the surrounding embeddings to come up with 
                                  the most relevant sense/meaning in current context (based on its surrounding words). 
                                  Based on the final embedding adjustment, there are probability assigned to each possible next word and sent to output
                                  layer. The output layer selects the probable words based on both probability and randomness and add it to the embeddings
                       
Generation                    --> This process repeats itself. The model will pause after generating each output token
                                  Then check - if we have reached a natural end of the response which is EOS (End of sequence token) 
                                  or the max token limit has reached

Output message will contain:
-------------------------------
Message: generated response text 
Usage: Number of input or output token
Stop Reason: EOS or max token limit reached or other that we will see in the topics later 

